  <p>There is no copy-length column. None of the eight collections carries a counted copy length for each platform, so the matrix has no copy axis rather than an invented one.</p>

  <p>Several rows need a sentence more than a cell allows.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">In the matrix, one core slot needs a wide frame that no square or vertical master serves cleanly: the WeChat Official Account lead cover. The most commonly published lead cover is 2.35:1 at 900 by 383 pixels, given by 7 of the 11 sources that state a size and by every one dated 2021 or later. Secondary articles in the same push take a 1:1 square, most often 200 by 200, on 8 of 10.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the WeChat collection, 12 Official Account domains, collected 10 September 2026 and grouped by date. Modal value, consensus, not a primary reading; "one core slot" is a count on this page's matrix.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">One vertical video master can go to WeChat Channels without a new frame. WeChat's Channels help center, read on 10 September 2026, recommends a standard 16:9 or 9:16 frame at 1080p or above and accepts any width-to-height ratio from 0.33 to 3.0.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the WeChat collection, Channels help-center article on posting requirements, served unauthenticated 10 September 2026, undated page. Primary reading, scoped to that article.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">WeChat's Mini Program framework reference, read on 10 September 2026, says a custom share image may be PNG or JPG and displays at a 5:4 aspect ratio. It gives no pixel size. A centered 5:4 cut from a square keeps the full width and the middle 80 percent of the height.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the WeChat collection, Page reference, onShareAppMessage imageUrl field, fetched 10 September 2026, undated page. Primary reading for the ratio and formats, scoped to the share image a Mini Program sets in code; the 80 percent is arithmetic.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Weibo's own video upload FAQ, updated 1 July 2024, gives a vertical video a 3:4 cover and a horizontal video a 16:9 or 4:3 cover, and does not allow a horizontal cover on a vertical video. For image posts of two to nine images, published sources agree the feed shows square tiles and do not agree on how a non-square image becomes one, so a square upload is the one shape no published account changes.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the Weibo collection: Weibo's help-center video FAQ dated 1 July 2024 (primary, scoped to that FAQ), and 4 non-platform references on the grid (2 fetched, 2 extract) collected 10 September 2026. The square-upload conclusion is that collection's derived safe intersection, not a platform rule.</p>

  <h2>Which China assets are re-crops and which need new capture?</h2>

  <p>Every slot falls into one of four kinds of work. The count below is one slot per distinct platform surface, for organic posts and marketplace listings. Rows marked optional in the matrix are left out.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Cell type</th>
          <th scope="col">What it means</th>
          <th scope="col">Slots</th>
          <th scope="col">Which</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Native capture</th>
          <td>The slot takes a frame at the ratio it was shot in; export only</td>
          <td>4</td>
          <td>Tmall white-background image, RedNote cover, RedNote inner images, Douyin post</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Re-crop</th>
          <td>The geometry changes, by a cut or a frame grab; nothing new is shot</td>
          <td>8</td>
          <td>Tmall main set, Douyin post cover, RedNote video cover, WeChat lead and secondary covers, Mini Program share card, Weibo grid images, Weibo video cover</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
