  <p>The capture sizes in the first two rows are production practice, not platform figures. A 1440 by 1920 still simply leaves a 1440 square in hand if the contested 1440 upgrade applies. The composition figures come from three derived boxes.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">On a 1080 by 1920 vertical master that also has to yield a RedNote cover, a Weibo cover and square tiles, one region clears every published figure used here: 108 to 972 pixels across and 420 to 1392 pixels down. It sits inside every Douyin inset in circulation, inside the center square of a centered 3:4 cut, and above the deepest bottom band published for RedNote covers.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: derived 10 September 2026 from the Douyin video collection (its Douyin safe box, from 5 Douyin sources), the RedNote cover collection (its center-square intersection across 10 sources and the 288-pixel bottom reservation, the largest of 8), and the Weibo collection (its square-upload intersection). Assumes the 3:4 and square cuts are centered, which no source states. A composition rule derived from published figures, not a platform rule.</p>

  <p>The box stops at 1392 rather than at the foot of the center square for a reason. On the most cautious published account, RedNote's interface overlay covers the lowest 15 to 20 percent of a cover, which reaches into the bottom of that square. The <a href="/resources/insights/rednote-note-cover-specs">RedNote cover specs article</a> made the same point in a correction dated 10 September 2026.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">On a 3:4 still of 1080 by 1440 pixels, keeping the product and any text inside the full-width band from 288 to 1152 pixels down lets one frame serve the RedNote cover, a centered square for Tmall, JD, Weibo and WeChat secondary covers, and a centered 5:4 Mini Program share card. The band is the middle 60 percent of the frame's height.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: derived 10 September 2026 from the RedNote cover collection (3:4 at 1080 by 1440 on 13; bottom reservation up to 288 pixels across 8), the Tmall product image collection (1:1 on 12 of 18), the JD against Tmall collection (1:1 on 6 of 6), the Weibo collection (square tiles on 4 references), and the WeChat collection (1:1 secondary cover on 11 of 11; 5:4 share image, primary, framework reference). Assumes centered cuts. Derived, not a platform rule.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">On a 1920 by 1080 landscape frame, a centered 2.35:1 cut for the WeChat lead cover keeps 817 pixels of height. Every published size for that cover's square crop equals the cover's own height, so the subject belongs in a centered square of about 816 by 816 pixels, roughly 552 to 1368 across and 132 to 948 down. The same square survives a 16:9 or a centered 4:3 Weibo horizontal video cover.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: derived 10 September 2026 from the WeChat collection (2.35:1 lead cover on 8 of 11; square-crop principle on 6, with published square sizes of 383, 500 and 766 each matching its cover height) and the Weibo collection (horizontal video cover 16:9 or 4:3, Weibo help-center FAQ dated 1 July 2024, primary, scoped). Assumes centered cuts. Derived, not a platform rule.</p>

  <p>The matrix does not ask whether a frame was photographed or generated, only whether it clears the box. hubStudio publishes its own split for ecommerce work.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">hubStudio publishes that most of its ecommerce catalogs run about 70 percent generated and 30 percent shot.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: hubStudio's ecommerce design service page, read 10 September 2026. A published first-party delivery mix, attributed to that page, not a measurement with a stated sample or period.</p>

  <p>The <a href="/solutions/ai-production/image">AI image production page</a> covers how those frames get made, the <a href="/solutions">solutions overview</a> covers the other production lanes, and the <a href="/work/diy-european-retailer">DIY European retailer case study</a> shows contextual imagery built across a large retailer's product catalog.</p>

  <p>Reviewed 10 September 2026. Check each platform's seller backend or creator account before shooting to any number on this page.</p>

  <h2>Questions about the China variant matrix</h2>

  <h3>How many assets does one SKU need for China?</h3>

  <p>No platform publishes a required count. Counted from the published figures on this page, one SKU across Tmall, JD, Douyin, RedNote, WeChat and Weibo fills 24 slots for organic posts and marketplace listings, or 27 with Douyin's paid formats, and 17 distinct files come out of the shoot. That is arithmetic with a stated scope, not a platform requirement.</p>

  <h3>Can one shoot serve Tmall, JD, Douyin and RedNote?</h3>

  <p>Yes, if the shoot covers four set-ups rather than one frame: a 3:4 still, a 9:16 video, a wide landscape frame and a white background. On the published figures, Tmall and JD take the same square set in a different order, and a centered 3:4 cut of a 1080 by 1920 frame is exactly the most published RedNote cover size.</p>

  <h3>Which China assets are re-crops and which need new capture?</h3>

  <p>Of the 24 core slots counted here, four take a frame at its captured ratio, eight are re-crops, eight re-use or re-export a file already made, and four are re-edits or page builds. The white-background image is the one listing asset a styled frame cannot supply, and Douyin's paid formats each need their own cut.</p>

  <h3>What has to be captured on the shoot day?</h3>

  <p>A 3:4 still at 1440 by 1920 or larger, a 9:16 video at 1080 by 1920 or larger with a clean three-second opening, a wide landscape frame for the WeChat lead cover, and a white-background set-up with one centered, front-view product. Add category close-ups, such as a food label, only where the category calls for them.</p>

  <h3>How many aspect ratios does a China launch need?</h3>

  <p>Five on this page's count: 1:1, 3:4, 9:16, 2.35:1 and 5:4, plus detail page and article body widths, which are builds rather than frames. Every square slot is a centered cut of the 3:4 still, so no separate square set-up is needed. Most of the ratios are commonly published values, not platform readings.</p>
